  'bed777fc-ed74-45b8-be38-1fd87e5d222d'::uuid,
  'moderate',
  '["Full Name","Section","Year of Study"]'::jsonb,
  '[{"question_text":"In the context of a database transaction, which property guarantees that once a transaction commits, its updates survive even if a sudden power failure occurs immediately after?","options":["The property of Atomicity","The property of Consistency","The property of Isolation","The property of Durability"],"correct_option_index":3,"option_explanations":["A treats a transaction as all-or-nothing. B ensures transitions between valid structural states. C isolates concurrent execution effects.","A treats a transaction as all-or-nothing. B ensures transitions between valid structural states. C isolates concurrent execution effects.","A treats a transaction as all-or-nothing. B ensures transitions between valid structural states. C isolates concurrent execution effects.","Durability ensures that the changes made by a successfully committed transaction are permanently written to non-volatile storage (like disk logs) and will not be lost during a crash."]},{"question_text":"Which relational algebra operator acts as a vertical filter to output a specific subset of columns while discarding the remaining attributes from the relation?","options":["Selection (represented by the Unicode symbol σ)","Projection (represented by the Unicode symbol π)","Intersection (represented by the Unicode symbol ∩)","Renaming (represented by the Unicode symbol ρ)"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A filters rows horizontally based on a conditional expression. C isolates rows common to two tables. D alters schema or attribute names.","The Projection operator (π) isolates a specific vertical slice of a relation by keeping named columns and automatically filtering out any duplicate tuples from the output set.","A filters rows horizontally based on a conditional expression. C isolates rows common to two tables. D alters schema or attribute names.","A filters rows horizontally based on a conditional expression. C isolates rows common to two tables. D alters schema or attribute names."]},{"question_text":"Consider the following query:\n\n```sql\nSELECT E1.Salary FROM Employees E1 WHERE 1 = (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT E2.Salary) FROM Employees E2 WHERE E2.Salary > E1.Salary);\n```\n\nWhat exactly does this query return?","options":["The absolute highest salary recorded across the entire table.","The second-highest salary value present within the table.","The lowest salary among employees with duplicate records.","The average salary of the top two earning employees."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A would require a count condition of 0. C and D describe completely different aggregate configurations that do not match this subquery structure.","The inner correlated subquery counts how many unique salaries are strictly greater than the outer salary (E1.Salary). If there is exactly 1 unique salary higher than E1.Salary, it must be the second-highest salary.","A would require a count condition of 0. C and D describe completely different aggregate configurations that do not match this subquery structure.","A would require a count condition of 0. C and D describe completely different aggregate configurations that do not match this subquery structure."]},{"question_text":"What happens if you completely omit the WHERE clause when executing a standard\n\n```sql\nUPDATE statement on a relational table?\n```","options":["The execution engine catches the omission and throws a syntax error.","The modifications are safely applied only to the first row of the table.","The column values for every single row in the table are overwritten.","The transaction is auto-suspended until a filter condition is provided."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A, B, and D are incorrect because SQL treats the lack of a filter as a valid request to update the entire dataset globally.","A, B, and D are incorrect because SQL treats the lack of a filter as a valid request to update the entire dataset globally.","Without a WHERE clause to restrict target records, the UPDATE statement executes globally, applying the new column modifications across every row in the dataset.","A, B, and D are incorrect because SQL treats the lack of a filter as a valid request to update the entire dataset globally."]},{"question_text":"What is the standard execution order of the foundational clauses within a database engine during a retrieval query?","options":["SELECT -> FROM -> WHERE -> GROUP BY","FROM -> WHERE -> GROUP BY -> SELECT","WHERE -> FROM -> GROUP BY -> SELECT","FROM -> SELECT -> WHERE -> GROUP BY"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D reflect incorrect execution paths that violate the operational processing rules of relational database optimizers.","The engine first locates the source data storage (FROM), filters individual rows (WHERE), clusters rows into groups (GROUP BY), and finally determines which columns to display (SELECT).","A, C, and D reflect incorrect execution paths that violate the operational processing rules of relational database optimizers.","A, C, and D reflect incorrect execution paths that violate the operational processing rules of relational database optimizers."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following conditions guarantees that a table decomposition into sub-relations R1 and R2 is a lossless-join decomposition?","options":["The intersection of R1 and R2 must yield an empty set.","The union of R1 and R2 must contain all original attributes.","The shared attributes must form a super key for R1 or R2.","Both sub-relations must contain the exact same primary key."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A creates a lossy join. B is a requirement for any decomposition but does not guarantee it is lossless. D is an unnecessary constraint.","A creates a lossy join. B is a requirement for any decomposition but does not guarantee it is lossless. D is an unnecessary constraint.","To reconstruct the original relation perfectly without generating incorrect, extra rows (spurious tuples), the common attributes between the split tables must uniquely identify rows in at least one of them.","A creates a lossy join. B is a requirement for any decomposition but does not guarantee it is lossless. D is an unnecessary constraint."]},{"question_text":"How do standard SQL aggregate functions (like SUM, AVG, MIN, and MAX) handle NULL values in a target column?","options":["They instantly halt execution and return a runtime error.","They convert the NULL values into numeric zeroes before calculating.","They omit the NULL values entirely from the calculation process.","They evaluate the entire final result of the column as NULL."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A, B, and D do not accurately reflect standard SQL optimization rules for handling empty fields during aggregation.","A, B, and D do not accurately reflect standard SQL optimization rules for handling empty fields during aggregation.","Standard SQL aggregate functions ignore missing data (NULL values) completely, performing their calculations using only rows that contain valid data.","A, B, and D do not accurately reflect standard SQL optimization rules for handling empty fields during aggregation."]},{"question_text":"Consider the tables Students(RollNo, Name) and Grades(RollNo, Grade). What does\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM Students NATURAL JOIN Grades;\n```\n\nreturn?","options":["A full cross-product displaying duplicate RollNo columns from both tables.","An inner join matched on the shared RollNo column, showing it only once.","A left outer join that preserves all student rows regardless of grade status.","A compilation error due to the missing ON or USING filter condition."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes a cross join. C describes an outer join. D is incorrect because natural joins implicitly determine their own match conditions without an ON clause.","A natural join (⋈) automatically looks for columns with identical names across both schemas, joins the tables based on equality in those columns, and removes duplicate lookup columns from the output.","A describes a cross join. C describes an outer join. D is incorrect because natural joins implicitly determine their own match conditions without an ON clause.","A describes a cross join. C describes an outer join. D is incorrect because natural joins implicitly determine their own match conditions without an ON clause."]},{"question_text":"What is the core structural difference between utilizing the IN operator versus the EXISTS operator in a conditional subquery?","options":["IN evaluates row existence; EXISTS performs a direct value scan.","IN scans data values; EXISTS verifies if the subquery returns rows.","IN is restricted to strings; EXISTS is restricted to numbers.","Both operators use identical internal validation routines in all engines."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is inverted. C is false because both operators work with any data type. D is incorrect because their optimization paths are fundamentally different.","The IN operator looks for explicit value matches against a list returned by the subquery. The EXISTS operator ignores the actual column values completely and simply checks if the subquery''s filters return any records at all.","A is inverted. C is false because both operators work with any data type. D is incorrect because their optimization paths are fundamentally different.","A is inverted. C is false because both operators work with any data type. D is incorrect because their optimization paths are fundamentally different."]},{"question_text":"In an Entity-Relationship (ER) diagram, what structural component is represented by a double-lined diamond?","options":["A relationship set that allows multi-valued attributes.","An identifying relationship linking a weak entity to a strong entity.","A recursive relationship mapping an entity set back to itself.","A total participation constraint enforced between strong entities."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D use different visual symbols (like standard lines, single diamonds, or loops) in classic Chen ER notation.","Weak entity sets cannot be identified by their own attributes alone and rely on a parent strong entity. The relationship that connects them is an identifying relationship, drawn as a double diamond.","A, C, and D use different visual symbols (like standard lines, single diamonds, or loops) in classic Chen ER notation.","A, C, and D use different visual symbols (like standard lines, single diamonds, or loops) in classic Chen ER notation."]},{"question_text":"If a database table satisfies Third Normal Form (3NF), what structural state is guaranteed?","options":["It is automatically guaranteed to satisfy Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF).","It has eliminated all partial dependencies but may contain transitive ones.","It has eliminated all transitive dependencies for non-prime attributes.","It completely eliminates the need for foreign key constraints across tables."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A is false because BCNF has stricter rules for key determinants. B describes 2NF. D is completely unrelated to normalization rules.","A is false because BCNF has stricter rules for key determinants. B describes 2NF. D is completely unrelated to normalization rules.","The primary goal of 3NF is to remove transitive dependencies, ensuring that no non-prime attribute relies on another non-prime attribute to map back to the primary key.","A is false because BCNF has stricter rules for key determinants. B describes 2NF. D is completely unrelated to normalization rules."]},{"question_text":"What occurs during a deletion anomaly in an unnormalized relational database table?","options":["Wiping out a record accidentally deletes completely unrelated information.","The execution engine blocks a deletion due to active concurrent access.","Dropping a row fails because the primary key field contains a null value.","The transaction log becomes corrupted and prevents future rollbacks."],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["A deletion anomaly happens when data is poorly organized. For example, if you delete the only student enrolled in a specific class, you might accidentally wipe out the class''s name and credit info because both facts are stored in the same row.","B describes a concurrency issue. C describes an entity integrity violation. D describes a hardware or disk crash scenario.","B describes a concurrency issue. C describes an entity integrity violation. D describes a hardware or disk crash scenario.","B describes a concurrency issue. C describes an entity integrity violation. D describes a hardware or disk crash scenario."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following SQL statements will cause an immediate execution error due to improper grouping rules?","options":["SELECT DeptID, AVG(Salary) FROM Employees GROUP BY DeptID;","SELECT DeptID, Salary FROM Employees GROUP BY DeptID;","SELECT DeptID FROM Employees GROUP BY DeptID HAVING AVG(Salary) > 50000;","SELECT DeptID, COUNT(*) FROM Employees GROUP BY DeptID, Role;"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D follow standard SQL syntax rules for aggregation and grouping.","In option B, Salary is requested in the SELECT list but is neither wrapped in an aggregate function (like SUM or AVG) nor listed in the GROUP BY clause. The engine cannot decide which individual worker''s salary to show for the grouped department row, throwing an error.","A, C, and D follow standard SQL syntax rules for aggregation and grouping.","A, C, and D follow standard SQL syntax rules for aggregation and grouping."]},{"question_text":"What is the absolute maximum number of designated Primary Keys that a single relational table can contain?","options":["It can contain zero primary keys.","It can contain exactly one primary key.","It can contain up to two primary keys.","It can contain an unlimited number of keys."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D violate foundational relational table design constraints.","A relational table can have at most one designated primary key. While it can feature multiple unique candidate keys, only one can be selected as the primary identifier.","A, C, and D violate foundational relational table design constraints.","A, C, and D violate foundational relational table design constraints."]},{"question_text":"Consider a table Inventory(ItemID, Price). What does\n\n```sql\nSELECT COUNT(*), COUNT(Price) FROM Inventory;\n```\n\nreturn if the table has 5 rows and one row has a NULL price?","options":["It outputs 5 and 5 because both count total records.","It outputs 5 and 4 because COUNT(column) skips nulls.","It outputs 4 and 4 because both skip rows with null fields.","It throws an execution error due to mixed counting styles."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D display incorrect row-counting logic and behavior regarding missing data values.","COUNT(*) counts every row in the table regardless of its contents, yielding 5. COUNT(Price) evaluates only the values within that specific column, skipping the NULL entry to yield 4.","A, C, and D display incorrect row-counting logic and behavior regarding missing data values.","A, C, and D display incorrect row-counting logic and behavior regarding missing data values."]},{"question_text":"The relational algebra operation known as a Theta Join is mathematically defined as which combination of basic operations?","options":["A Projection followed immediately by a Selection filter.","A Cartesian Product followed immediately by a Selection filter.","A Union operation followed immediately by a Renaming change.","A Set Difference followed immediately by a Projection filter."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D do not match the formal mathematical definitions of join mechanics in relational algebra.","A Theta Join combines two relations based on a general condition. It is logically equivalent to performing a full Cartesian cross-product (×) and then applying a specific selection condition (σ) to filter the matching rows.","A, C, and D do not match the formal mathematical definitions of join mechanics in relational algebra.","A, C, and D do not match the formal mathematical definitions of join mechanics in relational algebra."]},{"question_text":"Which command belongs to the Data Control Language (DCL) group and handles user access permissions?","options":["ALTER","COMMIT","REVOKE","UPDATE"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A is a DDL command. B is a TCL command. D is a DML command.","A is a DDL command. B is a TCL command. D is a DML command.","DCL commands manage security profiles and database privileges. REVOKE is used to remove previously granted access permissions from a user or role.","A is a DDL command. B is a TCL command. D is a DML command."]},{"question_text":"What does the inclusive BETWEEN operator evaluate when executed inside a standard SQL WHERE clause?","options":["Values strictly greater than the lower limit and less than the upper limit.","Values greater than or equal to the lower limit and less than or equal to the upper limit.","Values that match either the lower limit value or the upper limit value exclusively.","Values that fall outside the range bounded by the lower and upper limits."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes an exclusive range. C describes a discrete match check. D describes the behavior of a NOT BETWEEN filter.","In standard SQL syntax, the BETWEEN operator is fully inclusive. It matches values that are equal to the boundaries as well as everything that falls within them.","A describes an exclusive range. C describes a discrete match check. D describes the behavior of a NOT BETWEEN filter.","A describes an exclusive range. C describes a discrete match check. D describes the behavior of a NOT BETWEEN filter."]},{"question_text":"If a relation is in First Normal Form (1NF) and every single candidate key consists of only a single attribute, what can we conclude?","options":["The relation is guaranteed to satisfy Second Normal Form (2NF).","The relation is guaranteed to satisfy Third Normal Form (3NF).","The relation is guaranteed to satisfy Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF).","The relation violates basic relational database design principles."],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["Partial dependencies only occur when a non-prime attribute depends on a part (a proper subset) of a composite key. If all keys are single attributes, they have no proper subsets, making partial dependencies structurally impossible. Thus, it satisfies 2NF.","B and C can still be easily violated by transitive dependencies or non-key determinants, even with single-attribute keys. D is false.","B and C can still be easily violated by transitive dependencies or non-key determinants, even with single-attribute keys. D is false.","B and C can still be easily violated by transitive dependencies or non-key determinants, even with single-attribute keys. D is false."]},{"question_text":"In the three-schema database architecture, which specific schema level describes exactly how the data is physically organized and stored on the disk media?","options":["External Level","Conceptual Level","Internal Level","View Level"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A and D handle tailored user windows. B describes the complete global logical data model without physical specifications.","A and D handle tailored user windows. B describes the complete global logical data model without physical specifications.","The internal/physical level provides the lowest level of structural description, detailing block allocations, indices, and compression properties on raw storage hardware.","A and D handle tailored user windows. B describes the complete global logical data model without physical specifications."]},{"question_text":"If relation R contains 10 rows and relation S contains 5 rows, what is the exact number of rows returned by their Cartesian Product (R × S)?","options":["15 rows","5 rows","50 rows","2 rows"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A adds the counts. B subtracts them. D divides them.","A adds the counts. B subtracts them. D divides them.","The Cartesian Product (×) pairs every single row from the first table with every single row from the second table, resulting in a total row count equal to the product of the two source sizes (10 × 5 = 50).","A adds the counts. B subtracts them. D divides them."]},{"question_text":"Which SQL keyword is utilized to explicitly eliminate duplicate records from the output of a standard data retrieval query?","options":["UNIQUE","DISTINCT","SINGLE","ALL"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is used as a table structural constraint definition. C and D are incorrect syntax choices for duplicate filtering.","The DISTINCT keyword forces the database evaluation engine to filter out duplicate rows from the final query projection.","A is used as a table structural constraint definition. C and D are incorrect syntax choices for duplicate filtering.","A is used as a table structural constraint definition. C and D are incorrect syntax choices for duplicate filtering."]},{"question_text":"Given the relational schema R(A, B, C) and a functional dependency set containing A -> B and B -> C. What is the closure of the single attribute A?","options":["The set containing only {A}","","and a functional dependency set containing A -> B and B -> C. What is the closure of the single attribute A?A) The set containing only {A}B) The set containing only {A, B}C) The set containing all attributes {A, B, C}","The set containing only {B, C}"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A, B, and D fail to apply the transitive evaluation steps dictated by Armstrong''s Axioms.","A, B, and D fail to apply the transitive evaluation steps dictated by Armstrong''s Axioms.","Starting with A, A determines B via A -> B. Since B is now in the closure, it determines C via B -> C. This accumulates into the full set {A, B, C}.","A, B, and D fail to apply the transitive evaluation steps dictated by Armstrong''s Axioms."]},{"question_text":"What type of entity participation constraint dictates that every single instance within an entity set must be involved in a specific relationship set?","options":["Partial Participation","Total Participation","Optional Participation","Alternative Participation"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A and C mean some rows can remain unlinked. D is a placeholder term.","Total participation means that every row in the entity table must link to the relationship table. It is represented by a double line in ER diagrams.","A and C mean some rows can remain unlinked. D is a placeholder term.","A and C mean some rows can remain unlinked. D is a placeholder term."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following commands falls squarely under the Data Definition Language (DDL) taxonomy?","options":["INSERT","TRUNCATE","UPDATE","DELETE"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D are DML commands that modify row contents within active table frameworks.","TRUNCATE is a DDL command because it resets the underlying storage structures of the table by dropping and recreating its allocation pages, instantly clearing all data rows without row-by-row logging.","A, C, and D are DML commands that modify row contents within active table frameworks.","A, C, and D are DML commands that modify row contents within active table frameworks."]},{"question_text":"What does the EXISTS operator return if the nested subquery evaluates to an entirely empty result set?","options":["It evaluates to TRUE.","It evaluates to FALSE.","It evaluates to NULL.","It triggers a runtime crash."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D do not match the boolean validation design of the EXISTS operator.","EXISTS is a boolean indicator that returns TRUE if the inner query finds at least one matching row, and FALSE if the subquery returns no rows.","A, C, and D do not match the boolean validation design of the EXISTS operator.","A, C, and D do not match the boolean validation design of the EXISTS operator."]},{"question_text":"What condition must be met for two separate database relations to be compatible for set operations like Union and Intersection?","options":["They must contain the exact same number of data rows.","They must feature identical primary key column names.","They must have the same number of columns with matching data domains in the same order.","One table must be verified as a structural subset of the other."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A, B, and D are not rules for set operations on relations.","A, B, and D are not rules for set operations on relations.","Union compatibility requires that both relations have the same column count and that corresponding columns from left to right share matching data types.","A, B, and D are not rules for set operations on relations."]},{"question_text":"Consider a relation schema R(A, B, C, D) with the functional dependencies: AB -> C, C -> D, and D -> A. What are the prime attributes?","options":["The attributes {A, B}","The attributes {A, B, C}","","with the functional dependencies: AB -> C, C -> D, and D -> A. What are the prime attributes?A) The attributes {A, B}B) The attributes {A, B, C}C) The attributes {B, C, D}D) The attributes {A, B, C, D}"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A and C leave out valid prime columns. D incorrectly includes D, which is non-prime because it is not a member of any candidate key.","The candidate keys are AB, BC, and BD. Prime attributes are defined as any columns that appear in at least one candidate key. Combining the columns from all three keys yields {A, B, C}.","A and C leave out valid prime columns. D incorrectly includes D, which is non-prime because it is not a member of any candidate key.","A and C leave out valid prime columns. D incorrectly includes D, which is non-prime because it is not a member of any candidate key."]},{"question_text":"What does a double ellipse depict when analyzing a standard Chen-notation Entity-Relationship (ER) diagram?","options":["A key attribute","A derived attribute","A multi-valued attribute","A composite attribute"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A uses an underline. B uses a dashed ellipse. D branches out into sub-ellipses.","A uses an underline. B uses a dashed ellipse. D branches out into sub-ellipses.","Multi-valued attributes (like an employee with multiple phone numbers) are represented by double ellipses in standard ER diagrams.","A uses an underline. B uses a dashed ellipse. D branches out into sub-ellipses."]},{"question_text":"Which join type returns all records that match the join condition, plus unmatched rows from the left table filled with null values for the right table''s columns?","options":["Inner Join","Left Outer Join","Right Outer Join","Full Outer Join"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A drops unmatched rows. C preserves the right table. D preserves all unmatched rows from both tables.","A Left Outer Join preserves every row from the left table. If a row has no match in the right table, the right table''s columns are padded with NULL values.","A drops unmatched rows. C preserves the right table. D preserves all unmatched rows from both tables.","A drops unmatched rows. C preserves the right table. D preserves all unmatched rows from both tables."]},{"question_text":"In relational algebra, what is the exact behavior of the Set Difference operation (R - S)?","options":["It returns rows that are present in both relation R and relation S.","It returns rows that are present in relation R but absent in relation S.","It returns rows that are present in relation S but absent in relation R.","It returns all unique rows from both relations combined."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes an Intersection (∩). C describes S - R. D describes a Union (∪).","The Set Difference operator (-) extracts rows that exist in the left relation R but do not appear in the right relation S.","A describes an Intersection (∩). C describes S - R. D describes a Union (∪).","A describes an Intersection (∩). C describes S - R. D describes a Union (∪)."]},{"question_text":"What type of anomaly occurs when a user updates a piece of data that is duplicated across multiple rows, but the change fails to\n\n```sql\nupdate all copies?\n```","options":["Insertion Anomaly","Update Anomaly","Deletion Anomaly","Structural Anomaly"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A occurs when data cannot be added. C occurs when data is accidentally deleted. D is a placeholder term.","An update anomaly happens when data is duplicated due to poor normalization, meaning a simple modification requires updating multiple rows, which can easily lead to data inconsistencies.","A occurs when data cannot be added. C occurs when data is accidentally deleted. D is a placeholder term.","A occurs when data cannot be added. C occurs when data is accidentally deleted. D is a placeholder term."]},{"question_text":"Which integrity constraint explicitly dictates that no part of a primary key can accept a null value?","options":["Referential Integrity","Entity Integrity","Domain Constraint","Key Constraint"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A handles foreign keys. C restricts column data types and ranges. D ensures that candidate keys stay unique.","The Entity Integrity rule ensures that every row can be uniquely identified by completely banning primary key columns from accepting NULL values.","A handles foreign keys. C restricts column data types and ranges. D ensures that candidate keys stay unique.","A handles foreign keys. C restricts column data types and ranges. D ensures that candidate keys stay unique."]},{"question_text":"Under what condition can a database relation be classified as satisfying Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)?","options":["Every non-prime attribute is fully functionally dependent on the primary key.","It contains absolutely no transitive functional dependencies for non-prime attributes.","Every determinant in a non-trivial functional dependency must be a super key.","It allows composite candidate keys to overlap without restriction."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A describes 2NF. B describes 3NF. D is a scenario that BCNF explicitly corrects.","A describes 2NF. B describes 3NF. D is a scenario that BCNF explicitly corrects.","BCNF requires that for any non-trivial dependency X -> Y, the left-hand side determinant X must be a super key.","A describes 2NF. B describes 3NF. D is a scenario that BCNF explicitly corrects."]},{"question_text":"Consider the table query:\n\n```sql\nSELECT DeptID, COUNT(*) FROM Employees WHERE Salary > 40000 GROUP BY DeptID;\n```\n\nWhat rows are counted by the engine?","options":["Every single row present inside the table regardless of conditions.","Only the rows within each department where the worker''s salary is greater than 40000.","Only the rows belonging to departments that have a total salary greater than 40000.","No rows, because the query triggers a syntax error due to grouping rules."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A ignores the WHERE filter. C describes a HAVING clause filter. D is incorrect because the query syntax is valid.","The WHERE clause filters out individual rows with salaries of 40000 or less before any grouping happens. The remaining rows are then clustered by DeptID, and COUNT(*) counts the rows within each group.","A ignores the WHERE filter. C describes a HAVING clause filter. D is incorrect because the query syntax is valid.","A ignores the WHERE filter. C describes a HAVING clause filter. D is incorrect because the query syntax is valid."]},{"question_text":"The ability to modify the physical storage organization (such as indexing) without needing to rewrite the logical conceptual schema is called:","options":["Logical Data Independence","Physical Data Independence","Structural Data Independence","Conceptual Data Independence"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A isolates external applications from changes to the conceptual model. C and D are not standard terms.","Physical data independence isolates the conceptual layout from changes made to the underlying physical storage structures or file formats.","A isolates external applications from changes to the conceptual model. C and D are not standard terms.","A isolates external applications from changes to the conceptual model. C and D are not standard terms."]},{"question_text":"What does the wildcard symbol underscore (_) evaluate when utilized within a standard SQL LIKE pattern match?","options":["It matches any sequence of zero or more arbitrary characters.","It matches exactly one single arbitrary character.","It matches only literal numeric digits between 0 and 9.","It acts as an escape sequence for space characters."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes the percent (%) wildcard. C and D do not match the standard behavior of the underscore wildcard.","In SQL LIKE pattern matching, the underscore (_) matches exactly one character, while the % symbol matches any sequence of zero or more characters.","A describes the percent (%) wildcard. C and D do not match the standard behavior of the underscore wildcard.","A describes the percent (%) wildcard. C and D do not match the standard behavior of the underscore wildcard."]},{"question_text":"What type of binary relationship exists when an entity set is mapped into a relationship with itself?","options":["Binary Relationship","Ternary Relationship","Recursive Relationship","Symmetric Relationship"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A links two different entity sets. B links three entity sets. D is a mathematical term rather than a standard ER term.","A links two different entity sets. B links three entity sets. D is a mathematical term rather than a standard ER term.","When an entity participates in a relationship with itself (e.g., an employee table where workers report to another employee manager), it is a recursive or unary relationship.","A links two different entity sets. B links three entity sets. D is a mathematical term rather than a standard ER term."]},{"question_text":"If a database table is currently optimized to satisfy Second Normal Form (2NF), what structural issue has been completely eliminated?","options":["Transitive dependencies for non-prime attributes.","Partial dependencies on any composite candidate keys.","Multi-valued dependencies across independent columns.","Duplicate data rows sharing identical primary keys."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is addressed in 3NF. C is addressed in 4NF. D is enforced by basic primary key constraints.","The primary goal of 2NF is to eliminate partial dependencies, ensuring that no non-prime attribute depends on only a part of a composite candidate key.","A is addressed in 3NF. C is addressed in 4NF. D is enforced by basic primary key constraints.","A is addressed in 3NF. C is addressed in 4NF. D is enforced by basic primary key constraints."]},{"question_text":"Consider the table Employees(EmpID, Salary). What does\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM Employees WHERE Salary > ALL (SELECT Salary FROM Employees WHERE EmpID = 5);\n```\n\nreturn?","options":["Workers who earn more than every single employee in the company.","Workers who earn more than the employee whose ID is 5.","Workers who earn more than at least one employee in the company.","An empty result set due to an invalid use of the ALL operator."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D misinterpret how the > ALL operator works when compared against a single scalar value.","The subquery returns a single value: the salary of employee 5. The > ALL operator requires the outer salary to be greater than every value in the subquery''s result set, which means it must be strictly greater than employee 5''s salary.","A, C, and D misinterpret how the > ALL operator works when compared against a single scalar value.","A, C, and D misinterpret how the > ALL operator works when compared against a single scalar value."]},{"question_text":"Which relational algebra operator matches rows across two tables based on all columns that share identical names, automatically removing duplicate columns?","options":["Theta Join (represented by the symbol Join-Theta)","Natural Join (represented by the symbol Natural-Join)","Outer Join (represented by the symbol Outer-Join)","Cartesian Product (represented by the symbol Cross-Product)"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A uses an explicit join condition. C retains unmatched rows. D creates a cross-product of all rows without matching columns.","A natural join (⋈) automatically looks for columns with matching names across both schemas, joins the tables based on equality in those columns, and removes duplicate lookup columns from the output.","A uses an explicit join condition. C retains unmatched rows. D creates a cross-product of all rows without matching columns.","A uses an explicit join condition. C retains unmatched rows. D creates a cross-product of all rows without matching columns."]},{"question_text":"Which command belongs to the Data Control Language (DCL) taxonomy and is used to assign privileges to database users?","options":["ALTER","COMMIT","GRANT","UPDATE"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A is a DDL command. B is a TCL command. D is a DML command.","A is a DDL command. B is a TCL command. D is a DML command.","DCL commands manage user access and permissions. The GRANT command allows administrators to assign security privileges to specific users or roles.","A is a DDL command. B is a TCL command. D is a DML command."]},{"question_text":"What is the canonical cover of a set of functional dependencies?","options":["The absolute maximum set of all dependencies that can be derived.","A minimal equivalent set of dependencies containing no redundancies.","The group of primary keys that index the system files.","The query optimization plan generated by the database engine."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes a full closure set. C and D describe completely different database engineering concepts.","A canonical cover is the most stripped-down, efficient version of a set of functional dependencies that preserves the exact same closure properties without any redundancy.","A describes a full closure set. C and D describe completely different database engineering concepts.","A describes a full closure set. C and D describe completely different database engineering concepts."]},{"question_text":"What occurs when a database transaction enforces the property of Atomicity?","options":["Multiple concurrent users cannot access the same data rows.","All operations in a transaction succeed completely, or the entire transaction is aborted.","Changes made by a transaction are permanently saved even during crashes.","Data fields are restricted to holding only single, non-divisible values."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A relates to Isolation. C relates to Durability. D relates to first normal form (1NF) atomicity.","Atomicity is part of the ACID properties. It ensures that a transaction is treated as a single, indivisible unit: either all updates are committed, or the database is rolled back to its original state.","A relates to Isolation. C relates to Durability. D relates to first normal form (1NF) atomicity.","A relates to Isolation. C relates to Durability. D relates to first normal form (1NF) atomicity."]},{"question_text":"Consider the table Workers(ID, Name, Bonus). What does\n\n```sql\nSELECT COUNT(Bonus) FROM Workers;\n```\n\nreturn?","options":["The total number of rows present within the entire table.","The total number of rows where the Bonus column is non-NULL.","The sum of all numeric values stored inside the Bonus column.","A syntax error because COUNT requires the asterisk symbol."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is what COUNT(*) returns. C is the function of the SUM() command. D is incorrect because passing a column name to COUNT is valid standard syntax.","When COUNT() is given a specific column name as an argument, it counts only the rows that contain a non-NULL value in that column.","A is what COUNT(*) returns. C is the function of the SUM() command. D is incorrect because passing a column name to COUNT is valid standard syntax.","A is what COUNT(*) returns. C is the function of the SUM() command. D is incorrect because passing a column name to COUNT is valid standard syntax."]},{"question_text":"In an ER diagram, what type of attribute is represented visually by an ellipse containing an underlined name?","options":["A multi-valued attribute","A derived attribute","A primary key attribute","A composite attribute"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A uses a double ellipse. B uses a dashed ellipse. D branches out into sub-ellipses.","A uses a double ellipse. B uses a dashed ellipse. D branches out into sub-ellipses.","In standard Chen ER notation, a primary key attribute is represented by an ellipse containing the attribute''s name with a solid underline.","A uses a double ellipse. B uses a dashed ellipse. D branches out into sub-ellipses."]},{"question_text":"What type of query is created when an inner subquery references a column belonging to the outer query table?","options":["An independent subquery","A correlated subquery","A scalar subquery","An inline view query"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A executes completely independently of the outer loop. C returns a single value. D is a subquery placed inside the FROM clause.","A correlated subquery depends on the outer query because it references an outer column. This means it cannot be executed independently and must run repeatedly for each row processed by the outer loop.","A executes completely independently of the outer loop. C returns a single value. D is a subquery placed inside the FROM clause.","A executes completely independently of the outer loop. C returns a single value. D is a subquery placed inside the FROM clause."]},{"question_text":"If a relation R has 6 rows and relation S has 2 rows, what is the maximum number of rows that can be returned by the Set Difference operation (R - S)?","options":["6 rows","2 rows","8 rows","12 rows"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["The Set Difference operator (-) extracts rows that exist in the left relation R but do not appear in the right relation S. The largest possible result set occurs when the two tables share no rows, returning all 6 rows from R.","B applies to the inverted operation S - R. C and D represent upper limits for union and Cartesian product operations.","B applies to the inverted operation S - R. C and D represent upper limits for union and Cartesian product operations.","B applies to the inverted operation S - R. C and D represent upper limits for union and Cartesian product operations."]},{"question_text":"Which clause is used to filter aggregated group results after a GROUP BY clause has processed the rows?","options":["WHERE","HAVING","ORDER BY","SELECT"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A filters individual rows before any grouping happens. C sorts the final output rows. D specifies which columns to display.","The HAVING clause applies filter conditions to aggregated groups rather than individual records, which is why it can evaluate aggregate functions like COUNT() or SUM().","A filters individual rows before any grouping happens. C sorts the final output rows. D specifies which columns to display.","A filters individual rows before any grouping happens. C sorts the final output rows. D specifies which columns to display."]},{"question_text":"What is the primary difference between a database Schema and a database Instance?","options":["The schema changes frequently; the instance is permanent.","The schema is the structural design; the instance is the data snapshot at a moment in time.","The schema contains the data rows; the instance contains the metadata rules.","There is no structural or operational difference between them."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is inverted. C is incorrect because the schema holds the metadata, while the instance holds the data rows. D is false.","The schema is the structural blueprint designed before the database is populated and rarely changes. The instance is the actual operational data stored in the database at any given moment.","A is inverted. C is incorrect because the schema holds the metadata, while the instance holds the data rows. D is false.","A is inverted. C is incorrect because the schema holds the metadata, while the instance holds the data rows. D is false."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following conditions is an invalid configuration when using a standard SQL GROUP BY clause?","options":["Selecting an aggregate function alongside the grouped column.","Selecting a non-aggregated column that is absent from the GROUP BY clause.","Listing multiple columns separated by commas inside the grouping clause.","Sorting the final output rows by the grouped column using ORDER BY."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D follow standard SQL syntax rules for aggregation and grouping.","If a column is included in the SELECT clause but is not part of an aggregate function, it must be listed in the GROUP BY clause. Otherwise, the engine cannot map individual row values to the aggregated group, throwing an error.","A, C, and D follow standard SQL syntax rules for aggregation and grouping.","A, C, and D follow standard SQL syntax rules for aggregation and grouping."]},{"question_text":"Under Armstrong''s Axioms, if X -> Y and Y -> Z hold true, which rule allows us to derive X -> Z?","options":["Reflexivity Rule","Augmentation Rule","Transitivity Rule","Decomposition Rule"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A handles subset dependencies. B allows adding attributes to both sides. D splits a composite right-hand side.","A handles subset dependencies. B allows adding attributes to both sides. D splits a composite right-hand side.","The transitivity rule states that if X determines Y and Y determines Z, then X indirectly determines Z.","A handles subset dependencies. B allows adding attributes to both sides. D splits a composite right-hand side."]},{"question_text":"What does the UNION ALL set operator perform when combining the results of two SQL retrieval queries?","options":["It combines the rows vertically, automatically filtering out all duplicate rows.","It concatenates the result sets vertically, preserving all duplicate rows.","It links the data fields horizontally based on matching primary keys.","It extracts only the unique rows that appear in both queries."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes the standard UNION operator. C describes a join operation. D describes the INTERSECT operator.","Unlike the standard UNION operator, UNION ALL simply concatenates the query outputs without performing a distinct sort, preserving all duplicate records.","A describes the standard UNION operator. C describes a join operation. D describes the INTERSECT operator.","A describes the standard UNION operator. C describes a join operation. D describes the INTERSECT operator."]},{"question_text":"What type of table anomaly occurs when a user cannot record a new fact because it requires another missing piece of data?","options":["Update Anomaly","Insertion Anomaly","Deletion Anomaly","Modification Anomaly"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A and D occur when updating duplicate data requires changing multiple rows. C occurs when deleting a record accidentally removes unrelated data.","An insertion anomaly occurs when database constraints or poor table normalization prevent you from adding valid data (e.g., adding a new course before any students enroll in it if the primary key requires a student ID).","A and D occur when updating duplicate data requires changing multiple rows. C occurs when deleting a record accidentally removes unrelated data.","A and D occur when updating duplicate data requires changing multiple rows. C occurs when deleting a record accidentally removes unrelated data."]},{"question_text":"What is the fundamental property that distinguishes a Candidate Key from a general Super Key?","options":["A Candidate Key can accept null values, while a Super Key cannot.","A Candidate Key is a minimal Super Key that contains no redundant attributes.","A Super Key must contain every single column present inside the table.","A Candidate Key is automatically generated by the database engine."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is wrong; neither can violate uniqueness. C is wrong; super keys just need enough attributes to be unique. D is false.","While both super keys and candidate keys uniquely identify rows, a candidate key contains the absolute minimum set of columns needed for unique identification.","A is wrong; neither can violate uniqueness. C is wrong; super keys just need enough attributes to be unique. D is false.","A is wrong; neither can violate uniqueness. C is wrong; super keys just need enough attributes to be unique. D is false."]},{"question_text":"Consider the table query:\n\n```sql\nSELECT Name FROM Products WHERE Price >= ALL (SELECT Price FROM Products);\n```\n\nWhat does this return?","options":["The names of every single product recorded in the database.","The name of the product that has the absolute highest price.","The names of products that cost more than the average price.","A syntax error because aggregate functions are missing."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A requires an unconditional query. C describes comparing against an AVG() subquery. D is incorrect because this is valid SQL syntax.","The >=\\text{ ALL} operator requires the outer Price value to be greater than or equal to every individual value returned by the subquery, which effectively identifies the maximum value in the column.","A requires an unconditional query. C describes comparing against an AVG() subquery. D is incorrect because this is valid SQL syntax.","A requires an unconditional query. C describes comparing against an AVG() subquery. D is incorrect because this is valid SQL syntax."]},{"question_text":"In an ER diagram, how is a weak entity set (which lacks a primary key of its own) represented visually?","options":["A double-lined ellipse","A dashed-lined ellipse","A double-lined rectangle","A double-lined diamond"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A represents a multi-valued attribute. B represents a derived attribute. D represents the identifying relationship linking a weak entity to a strong entity.","A represents a multi-valued attribute. B represents a derived attribute. D represents the identifying relationship linking a weak entity to a strong entity.","In standard Chen ER notation, a weak entity set—which lacks a primary key of its own and depends on a strong entity—is enclosed in a double-lined rectangle.","A represents a multi-valued attribute. B represents a derived attribute. D represents the identifying relationship linking a weak entity to a strong entity."]},{"question_text":"Which relational algebra operator acts as a horizontal filter to output a subset of rows based on a given predicate condition?","options":["Selection (represented by the Unicode symbol σ)","Projection (represented by the Unicode symbol π)","Rename (represented by the Unicode symbol ρ)","Join (represented by the Unicode symbol ⋈)"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["The Selection operator (σ) acts as a horizontal filter, extracting rows from a relation that meet a specific condition while preserving all original columns.","B filters columns vertically. C changes column or table identifiers. D merges two tables based on a condition.","B filters columns vertically. C changes column or table identifiers. D merges two tables based on a condition.","B filters columns vertically. C changes column or table identifiers. D merges two tables based on a condition."]},{"question_text":"What happens if a subquery evaluated inside an equality comparison operator (like =) returns more than one row at runtime?","options":["The engine automatically selects the first row and discards the rest.","The database engine halts execution and throws a runtime error.","The query converts the operator into an implicit IN condition.","The comparison evaluates to null for all outer rows."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D do not reflect how standard SQL engines handle cardinality mismatches with scalar operators.","A scalar comparison operator like = expects a single value. If the subquery returns multiple rows, the comparison becomes ambiguous, causing the database engine to throw a runtime error.","A, C, and D do not reflect how standard SQL engines handle cardinality mismatches with scalar operators.","A, C, and D do not reflect how standard SQL engines handle cardinality mismatches with scalar operators."]},{"question_text":"If a relation schema R has no composite candidate keys, which normal form is automatically satisfied if the table is already in 1NF?","options":["Second Normal Form (2NF)","Third Normal Form (3NF)","Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)","Fourth Normal Form (4NF)"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["Partial dependencies only occur when a non-prime attribute depends on a part (a proper subset) of a composite key. If all keys are single attributes, they have no proper subsets, making partial dependencies structurally impossible. Thus, it satisfies 2NF.","B, C, and D can still be easily violated by transitive dependencies or non-key determinants, even with single-attribute keys.","B, C, and D can still be easily violated by transitive dependencies or non-key determinants, even with single-attribute keys.","B, C, and D can still be easily violated by transitive dependencies or non-key determinants, even with single-attribute keys."]},{"question_text":"What is the default sorting order when you use the ORDER BY clause in an SQL query?","options":["Descending order (DESC)","","B) Ascending order (ASC)C) A random order based on physical disk layout","Sorted automatically by the primary key columns"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A must be explicitly declared. C and D do not reflect the predictable sorting behavior of the ORDER BY clause.","By default, the ORDER BY clause sorts results in ascending order (ASC) unless you explicitly append the DESC keyword.","A must be explicitly declared. C and D do not reflect the predictable sorting behavior of the ORDER BY clause.","A must be explicitly declared. C and D do not reflect the predictable sorting behavior of the ORDER BY clause."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following check routines is violated if a user updates a foreign key to a value that is missing from the referenced parent table?","options":["Entity Integrity Constraint","Domain Integrity Constraint","Referential Integrity Constraint","Key Constraint Verification"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A deals with null values in primary keys. B deals with data type or value ranges. D ensures that candidate keys stay unique.","A deals with null values in primary keys. B deals with data type or value ranges. D ensures that candidate keys stay unique.","Referential integrity ensures that links between tables remain consistent. A foreign key value must always point to a valid, existing row in the parent table, or be set to NULL.","A deals with null values in primary keys. B deals with data type or value ranges. D ensures that candidate keys stay unique."]},{"question_text":"In relational algebra, what is the behavior of the Set Intersection operation (R ∩ S)?","options":["It returns rows that appear in either relation R or relation S.","It returns rows that appear in both relation R and relation S simultaneously.","It returns rows that appear in relation R but are absent from relation S.","It matches columns horizontally based on shared names."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes a Union (∪). C describes a Set Difference (-). D describes a join operation.","The Set Intersection operator (∩) extracts only the rows that are shared between both relations, provided the tables are union-compatible.","A describes a Union (∪). C describes a Set Difference (-). D describes a join operation.","A describes a Union (∪). C describes a Set Difference (-). D describes a join operation."]},{"question_text":"Consider the table Students(ID, Name, Age). What is the primary difference between executing\n\n```sql\nDROP TABLE Students;\n```\n\nand TRUNCATE TABLE Students;?","options":["DROP removes specific rows; TRUNCATE deletes all rows.","DROP deletes both the data and the structural definition; TRUNCATE deletes the data rows but leaves the empty structure intact.","TRUNCATE can be rolled back; DROP cannot be rolled back.","There is no structural difference between their behaviors."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes a conditional DELETE statement. C is incorrect because DDL commands auto-commit instantly in most standard SQL engines, making them difficult to roll back compared to DML operations. D is false.","DROP completely deletes both the data rows and the metadata definition of the table from the database catalog. TRUNCATE acts as a fast DDL clear operation that wipes the data rows but keeps the table skeleton for future insertions.","A describes a conditional DELETE statement. C is incorrect because DDL commands auto-commit instantly in most standard SQL engines, making them difficult to roll back compared to DML operations. D is false.","A describes a conditional DELETE statement. C is incorrect because DDL commands auto-commit instantly in most standard SQL engines, making them difficult to roll back compared to DML operations. D is false."]},{"question_text":"Given a relation R(A, B, C) and its active rows: (1, 2, 3), (1, 2, 4), and (2, 3, 5). Which dependency holds true?","options":["The functional dependency A -> B","","and its active rows: (1, 2, 3), (1, 2, 4), and (2, 3, 5). Which dependency holds true?A) The functional dependency A -> BB) The functional dependency B -> CC) The functional dependency A -> C","None of the listed functional dependencies"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["$A \\rightarrow B$ states that matching values in column $A$ must have matching values in column $B$. In this instance, when $A = 1$, $B = 2$ in both rows, and when $A = 2$, $B = 3$. No rows violate this rule.","B is violated because when $B = 2$, $C$ has two different values (3 and 4). C is violated because when $A = 1$, $C$ has two different values (3 and 4).","B is violated because when $B = 2$, $C$ has two different values (3 and 4). C is violated because when $A = 1$, $C$ has two different values (3 and 4).","B is violated because when $B = 2$, $C$ has two different values (3 and 4). C is violated because when $A = 1$, $C$ has two different values (3 and 4)."]},{"question_text":"What does a single ellipse branching out into multiple sub-ellipses represent in an ER diagram?","options":["A multi-valued attribute","A derived attribute","A composite attribute","A primary key attribute"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A uses a double ellipse. B uses a dashed ellipse. D uses an underline.","A uses a double ellipse. B uses a dashed ellipse. D uses an underline.","Composite attributes (like Address, which breaks down into Street, City, and ZipCode) are represented by a main attribute ellipse connected to multiple component ellipses.","A uses a double ellipse. B uses a dashed ellipse. D uses an underline."]},{"question_text":"What does a RIGHT OUTER JOIN ($A \\text{ RIGHT JOIN } B$) do if a row in the right table B has no match in the left table A?","options":["The unmatched row from table B is excluded from the output.","The query aborts execution and logs a constraint failure.","The row from table B is included in the output, and all columns from table A are filled with NULL.","The missing left table data is replaced with zeroes."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A describes the behavior of an INNER JOIN. B and D do not reflect standard outer join behavior.","A describes the behavior of an INNER JOIN. B and D do not reflect standard outer join behavior.","A Right Outer Join guarantees that every row from the right table appears in the final result. If a row has no match in the left table, the left table''s columns are filled with NULL placeholders.","A describes the behavior of an INNER JOIN. B and D do not reflect standard outer join behavior."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following statements correctly defines a prime attribute in database normalization theory?","options":["An attribute that contains only prime numbers.","An attribute that is a member of at least one candidate key.","An attribute that completely forbids null values.","An attribute that is automatically indexed by the system."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is a numerical joke. C describes a primary key constraint, but prime attributes from other candidate keys might allow nulls depending on the configuration. D is an operational choice, not a definition.","Attributes that are part of any candidate key are called prime attributes. All other attributes are non-prime.","A is a numerical joke. C describes a primary key constraint, but prime attributes from other candidate keys might allow nulls depending on the configuration. D is an operational choice, not a definition.","A is a numerical joke. C describes a primary key constraint, but prime attributes from other candidate keys might allow nulls depending on the configuration. D is an operational choice, not a definition."]},{"question_text":"What value does the query\n\n```sql\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM Customers;\n```\n\nreturn if the table contains 6 rows, and two rows have null fields across all columns except the primary key?","options":["It returns a count value of 4 rows.","It returns a count value of 6 rows.","It returns a count value of 0 rows.","It triggers an immediate runtime execution error."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A would be the result if a specific column containing those null values was passed to the function instead of *. C and D do not reflect standard SQL behavior.","COUNT(*) counts the total number of rows in a table, regardless of whether individual columns contain NULL values.","A would be the result if a specific column containing those null values was passed to the function instead of *. C and D do not reflect standard SQL behavior.","A would be the result if a specific column containing those null values was passed to the function instead of *. C and D do not reflect standard SQL behavior."]},{"question_text":"Consider the table Sales(EmpID, Year, Revenue). What does\n\n```sql\nSELECT EmpID FROM Sales WHERE Year = 2026 INTERSECT SELECT EmpID FROM Sales WHERE Year = 2027;\n```\n\nreturn?","options":["Employees who recorded sales in either 2026 or 2027.","Employees who recorded sales in 2026 but skipped 2027.","Employees who recorded sales in both 2026 and 2027.","A cross-product of all employee records across both years."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A describes the UNION operator. B describes the EXCEPT operator. D describes a cross join.","A describes the UNION operator. B describes the EXCEPT operator. D describes a cross join.","The INTERSECT operator returns only the unique values that appear in the results of both the first and second queries.","A describes the UNION operator. B describes the EXCEPT operator. D describes a cross join."]},{"question_text":"What does the inclusive comparison expression WHERE column_name IS NULL do?","options":["It returns rows containing a literal string matching ''NULL''.","It accurately filters and returns rows where that column value is missing.","It always evaluates to unknown and returns an empty set.","It causes a syntax error because the equality sign is missing."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A requires quoting the string. C and D describe the incorrect use of standard comparison operators (like = NULL), which do fail or return empty sets.","In SQL, NULL represents the absence of a value rather than a specific value. Because of this, you cannot use standard comparison operators like = or ==. Instead, you must use the special operators IS NULL or IS NOT NULL.","A requires quoting the string. C and D describe the incorrect use of standard comparison operators (like = NULL), which do fail or return empty sets.","A requires quoting the string. C and D describe the incorrect use of standard comparison operators (like = NULL), which do fail or return empty sets."]},{"question_text":"Which normal form is strictly designed to eliminate transitive functional dependencies for non-prime attributes?","options":["First Normal Form (1NF)","Second Normal Form (2NF)","Third Normal Form (3NF)","Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A handles atomicity. B handles partial dependencies. D handles all key determinant variations.","A handles atomicity. B handles partial dependencies. D handles all key determinant variations.","The primary goal of 3NF is to remove transitive dependencies, ensuring that no non-prime attribute relies on another non-prime attribute to map back to the primary key.","A handles atomicity. B handles partial dependencies. D handles all key determinant variations."]},{"question_text":"If relation R has a degree of 3 and relation S has a degree of 2, what is the degree of the relation produced by their Cartesian Product (R × S)?","options":["A degree of 6","A degree of 5","A degree of 3","A degree of 2"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A multiplies the columns instead of adding them. C and D just select the degree of one of the tables.","The degree of a relation is the number of columns it has. The degree of a Cartesian product is the sum of the degrees of the two input relations (3 + 2 = 5).","A multiplies the columns instead of adding them. C and D just select the degree of one of the tables.","A multiplies the columns instead of adding them. C and D just select the degree of one of the tables."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following query targets represents a virtual table that saves an SQL statement in the data dictionary without storing duplicate data rows on the disk?","options":["A materialized view table","A database view structure","A partition block backup","An index structure link"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes a view that physically caches data on disk. C and D describe different administrative utilities.","Views are virtual tables. They store the underlying query definition in the system catalog rather than storing duplicate data on disk. When you query a view, it dynamically retrieves the latest data from the base tables.","A describes a view that physically caches data on disk. C and D describe different administrative utilities.","A describes a view that physically caches data on disk. C and D describe different administrative utilities."]},{"question_text":"What occurs during a deletion anomaly in a poorly normalized database table?","options":["Modifying an attribute value fails to update duplicate records.","Dropping a row accidentally removes unrelated information that should have been preserved.","Adding a new record is blocked due to a missing primary key.","The execution engine deadlocks due to active transaction blocks."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes an update anomaly. C describes an insertion anomaly. D describes a transaction concurrency issue.","A deletion anomaly happens when data is poorly organized. For example, if you delete the only student enrolled in a specific class, you might accidentally wipe out the class''s name and credit info because both facts are stored in the same row.","A describes an update anomaly. C describes an insertion anomaly. D describes a transaction concurrency issue.","A describes an update anomaly. C describes an insertion anomaly. D describes a transaction concurrency issue."]},{"question_text":"Consider the table Products(ID, Name, Price). What does\n\n```sql\nSELECT Name FROM Products WHERE Price BETWEEN 20 AND 50;\n```\n\nevaluate?","options":["Products with a price strictly greater than 20 and less than 50.","Products with a price greater than or equal to 20 and less than or equal to 50.","Products with a price matching exactly 20 or exactly 50 only.","A syntax error because the range requires separate comparison signs."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes an exclusive range. C describes a discrete match check. D is false because BETWEEN is standard SQL syntax.","In standard SQL syntax, the BETWEEN operator is fully inclusive. It matches values that are equal to the boundaries as well as everything that falls within them.","A describes an exclusive range. C describes a discrete match check. D is false because BETWEEN is standard SQL syntax.","A describes an exclusive range. C describes a discrete match check. D is false because BETWEEN is standard SQL syntax."]},{"question_text":"What condition must be met for a non-trivial functional dependency X -> Y to satisfy the requirements of Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)?","options":["The attribute Y must be verified as a prime attribute.","The determinant X must be verified as a super key.","The determinant X must consist of only a single attribute.","The attributes X and Y must share at least one column."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is allowed in 3NF but banned in BCNF. C is an unnecessary restriction. D introduces an irrelevant structural rule.","BCNF requires that for every non-trivial functional dependency X -> Y, the left-hand side determinant X must be a super key for the relation.","A is allowed in 3NF but banned in BCNF. C is an unnecessary restriction. D introduces an irrelevant structural rule.","A is allowed in 3NF but banned in BCNF. C is an unnecessary restriction. D introduces an irrelevant structural rule."]},{"question_text":"Which level of the three-schema database architecture provides tailored views of the database to individual users or applications?","options":["External Level","Conceptual Level","Internal Level","Physical Level"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["The external level (or view level) describes part of the database that is relevant to a specific user group, masking the rest of the database logic.","B describes the global structural data model. C and D handle physical file storage setups on hardware.","B describes the global structural data model. C and D handle physical file storage setups on hardware.","B describes the global structural data model. C and D handle physical file storage setups on hardware."]},{"question_text":"Consider the table Workers(EmpID, Name, JobRole). What does\n\n```sql\nSELECT JobRole, COUNT(*) FROM Workers GROUP BY JobRole;\n```\n\nreturn?","options":["The total number of rows in the table repeated across roles.","A list of unique job roles along with the number of workers in each role.","A list of worker names organized alphabetically by role.","A syntax error because COUNT cannot group text values."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A misses the grouping context. C describes a sorting operation. D is false because grouping by text columns is standard practice.","The query groups the rows by the JobRole column and then counts the number of records within each group, showing the total number of workers per role.","A misses the grouping context. C describes a sorting operation. D is false because grouping by text columns is standard practice.","A misses the grouping context. C describes a sorting operation. D is false because grouping by text columns is standard practice."]},{"question_text":"In an ER diagram, what constraint is depicted by drawing a double line between an entity set and a relationship set?","options":["Partial Participation Constraint","Total Participation Constraint","Unique Primary Key Constraint","Multivalued Attribute Link"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is represented by a single line. C and D use different lines and symbols to indicate cardinality and unique constraints.","Total participation means that every entity in the set must participate in at least one relationship instance within that relationship set. This is represented visually by a double line.","A is represented by a single line. C and D use different lines and symbols to indicate cardinality and unique constraints.","A is represented by a single line. C and D use different lines and symbols to indicate cardinality and unique constraints."]},{"question_text":"Which relational algebra operator matches every row of the first relation with every row of the second relation?","options":["Natural Join (represented by the symbol Natural-Join)","Cartesian Product (represented by the symbol Cross-Product)","Projection (represented by the symbol Project)","Set Difference (represented by the symbol Minus)"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A only matches rows that share equal values in columns with matching names. C filters columns. D calculates set difference.","The Cartesian product (×) matches every row of the left table with every row of the right table, resulting in a total row count equal to the product of the two input row counts.","A only matches rows that share equal values in columns with matching names. C filters columns. D calculates set difference.","A only matches rows that share equal values in columns with matching names. C filters columns. D calculates set difference."]},{"question_text":"What does the\n\n```sql\nTRUNCATE TABLE command do behind the scenes that makes it faster than a standard DELETE FROM query?\n```","options":["It bypasses data type validation checks.","It drops and recreates the table storage allocation to minimize log overhead.","It uses multi-threading to delete rows in parallel blocks.","It converts the table into a read-only temporary view."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D are incorrect descriptions that do not reflect how TRUNCATE operates.","TRUNCATE is a DDL command that deallocates the data pages associated with a table, bypassing row-by-row logging. This makes it significantly faster than DELETE when clearing large tables.","A, C, and D are incorrect descriptions that do not reflect how TRUNCATE operates.","A, C, and D are incorrect descriptions that do not reflect how TRUNCATE operates."]},{"question_text":"Consider a table Salaries(EmpID, Year, Amount). What does\n\n```sql\nSELECT EmpID FROM Salaries WHERE Year = 2026 EXCEPT SELECT EmpID FROM Salaries WHERE Year = 2027;\n```\n\nreturn?","options":["Employees who worked in either 2026 or 2027.","Employees who worked in 2026 but did not work in 2027.","Employees who worked in both 2026 and 2027.","A cross-product of all employee records across both years."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes the UNION operator. C describes the INTERSECT operator. D describes a cross join.","The EXCEPT operator calculates the set difference, returning rows from the first query that are not present in the second query''s results.","A describes the UNION operator. C describes the INTERSECT operator. D describes a cross join.","A describes the UNION operator. C describes the INTERSECT operator. D describes a cross join."]},{"question_text":"In an ER diagram, what does an ellipse with a dashed boundary line represent?","options":["A key attribute","A derived attribute","A multi-valued attribute","A composite attribute"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A uses a solid underline. C uses a double ellipse. D branches out into sub-ellipses.","Derived attributes (like Age, which can be calculated from a DateOfBirth column) are represented visually using dashed ellipses.","A uses a solid underline. C uses a double ellipse. D branches out into sub-ellipses.","A uses a solid underline. C uses a double ellipse. D branches out into sub-ellipses."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following SQL statements will cause an immediate execution error due to improper use of an aggregate function?","options":["SELECT DeptID, AVG(Salary) FROM Employees GROUP BY DeptID;","SELECT DeptID, AVG(Salary) FROM Employees WHERE Salary > 30000 GROUP BY DeptID;","SELECT DeptID, AVG(Salary) FROM Employees WHERE AVG(Salary) > 50000 GROUP BY DeptID;","SELECT DeptID FROM Employees GROUP BY DeptID HAVING AVG(Salary) > 50000;"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A, B, and D follow standard SQL syntax rules for aggregation, grouping, and filtering.","A, B, and D follow standard SQL syntax rules for aggregation, grouping, and filtering.","In option C, the aggregate function AVG(Salary) is placed inside the WHERE clause. This causes an error because the WHERE clause filters individual rows before any grouping or aggregation happens. To filter based on aggregate values, you must use the HAVING clause.","A, B, and D follow standard SQL syntax rules for aggregation, grouping, and filtering."]},{"question_text":"If a relation is in Second Normal Form (2NF) and contains no transitive functional dependencies, what is its highest normal form?","options":["First Normal Form (1NF) only","Third Normal Form (3NF)","Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)","Fourth Normal Form (4NF)"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is not the highest normal form satisfied. C, D, and advanced normal forms require additional constraints beyond the removal of transitive dependencies.","By definition, a relation is in Third Normal Form (3NF) if it satisfies 2NF and contains no transitive dependencies for non-prime attributes.","A is not the highest normal form satisfied. C, D, and advanced normal forms require additional constraints beyond the removal of transitive dependencies.","A is not the highest normal form satisfied. C, D, and advanced normal forms require additional constraints beyond the removal of transitive dependencies."]},{"question_text":"Given tables Students(RollNo, Name) and Grades(RollNo, Grade). What type of join is performed by\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM Students NATURAL JOIN Grades;\n```\n\n?","options":["A full cross product containing two separate RollNo columns.","An inner join that matches rows based on the shared RollNo column, showing it only once.","A left outer join that preserves all student rows regardless of grade status.","A syntax error because the query lacks an explicit ON clause."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A retains duplicate columns. C describes an outer join. D is incorrect because NATURAL JOIN does not use an ON clause.","A NATURAL JOIN implicitly matches rows based on columns with identical names across both tables (here, RollNo) and automatically simplifies the output schema by combining them into a single column.","A retains duplicate columns. C describes an outer join. D is incorrect because NATURAL JOIN does not use an ON clause.","A retains duplicate columns. C describes an outer join. D is incorrect because NATURAL JOIN does not use an ON clause."]},{"question_text":"Which SQL command belongs to the Data Control Language (DCL) and is used to remove previously granted access privileges?","options":["DENY","REVOKE","ROLLBACK","DROP"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is used in specific SQL variations but REVOKE is the standard command. C is a TCL command. D is a DDL command.","REVOKE is a DCL command used to remove security privileges or permissions from a database user or role.","A is used in specific SQL variations but REVOKE is the standard command. C is a TCL command. D is a DDL command.","A is used in specific SQL variations but REVOKE is the standard command. C is a TCL command. D is a DDL command."]},{"question_text":"What does the expression Selection (using the condition Age > 21) represent in relational algebra?","options":["A projection that extracts the Age column from relation R.","A selection that filters and returns all rows from relation R where Age is greater than 21.","A rename operation that updates the table name to 21.","A join condition linking relation R to another table."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes a projection (π). C describes a rename (ρ). D describes a join (⋈).","The selection operator (σ) acts as a horizontal filter to return rows that meet a specific condition while preserving all original columns.","A describes a projection (π). C describes a rename (ρ). D describes a join (⋈).","A describes a projection (π). C describes a rename (ρ). D describes a join (⋈)."]},{"question_text":"What happens if a subquery evaluated inside an IN clause returns an empty result set?","options":["The comparison automatically evaluates to TRUE.","The comparison automatically evaluates to FALSE.","The comparison automatically evaluates to NULL.","The database engine throws a runtime syntax error."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is incorrect since there are no matching values. C and D do not reflect how SQL handles empty sets in standard membership comparisons.","If a subquery returns an empty result set, the IN condition cannot find a match for the outer value, causing the expression to evaluate to FALSE.","A is incorrect since there are no matching values. C and D do not reflect how SQL handles empty sets in standard membership comparisons.","A is incorrect since there are no matching values. C and D do not reflect how SQL handles empty sets in standard membership comparisons."]},{"question_text":"Which normal form requires all attribute domains to contain only atomic values, banning repeating groups and nested tables?","options":["First Normal Form (1NF)","Second Normal Form (2NF)","Third Normal Form (3NF)","Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["First Normal Form (1NF) establishes the core baseline of the relational model: every column must contain atomic values, and multi-valued fields or repeating groups are forbidden.","B, C, and BCNF assume the data satisfies 1NF and introduce additional constraints to address advanced dependency issues.","B, C, and BCNF assume the data satisfies 1NF and introduce additional constraints to address advanced dependency issues.","B, C, and BCNF assume the data satisfies 1NF and introduce additional constraints to address advanced dependency issues."]},{"question_text":"Consider the table Products(ID, Name, Category, Price). What does\n\n```sql\nSELECT Category, MIN(Price) FROM Products WHERE Price > 10 GROUP BY Category;\n```\n\nreturn?","options":["The minimum price across all categories combined, provided it is greater than 10.","A list of categories along with the minimum price within each category, considering only products that cost more than 10.","All product rows where the price is greater than 10, sorted by category.","A syntax error because WHERE cannot be used before a GROUP BY clause."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A misses the per-category grouping. C describes a standard filtered query without aggregation. D is false because WHERE always runs before GROUP BY.","The WHERE clause filters out products with a price of 10 or less first. The remaining rows are then grouped by category, and the MIN() function finds the lowest price within each group.","A misses the per-category grouping. C describes a standard filtered query without aggregation. D is false because WHERE always runs before GROUP BY.","A misses the per-category grouping. C describes a standard filtered query without aggregation. D is false because WHERE always runs before GROUP BY."]},{"question_text":"In an ER diagram, what is a partial key (or discriminator), and how is it represented visually?","options":["A key that uniquely identifies a strong entity, represented by a bold line.","An attribute used to distinguish weak entities that depend on the same strong owner record, represented by a dashed underline inside an ellipse.","A foreign key pointer, represented by a double diamond symbol.","An attribute that can accept null values, represented by a dashed ellipse."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes a primary key. C and D describe different ER components.","A weak entity set does not have a primary key. It relies on a combination of its parent strong entity''s primary key and its own partial key (discriminator), which is marked by a dashed underline.","A describes a primary key. C and D describe different ER components.","A describes a primary key. C and D describe different ER components."]},{"question_text":"Given two relations R(A, B) and S(B, C), how many columns will be present in the relation produced by their full Cartesian Product (R × S)?","options":["","and S(B,",", how many columns will be present in the relation produced by their full Cartesian Product (R × S)?A) It will contain a total of 2 columns.B) It will contain a total of 3 columns.C) It will contain a total of 4 columns.","It will contain a total of 0 columns."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A and B underestimate the column count because they don''t combine both schemas fully. D is incorrect.","A and B underestimate the column count because they don''t combine both schemas fully. D is incorrect.","The Cartesian product horizontally combines the schemas of both tables, meaning the resulting output contains all columns from both the left and right tables combined (2 + 2 = 4).","A and B underestimate the column count because they don''t combine both schemas fully. D is incorrect."]},{"question_text":"What does the EXISTS operator evaluate in a SQL WHERE clause?","options":["It verifies whether a specific column value matches any value in a list.","It checks whether the nested subquery returns at least one row, returning TRUE as soon as a record is found.","It checks whether a table exists in the system catalog.","It calculates the average number of rows in a table group."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes the IN operator. C describes a data dictionary check. D is an aggregate function.","The EXISTS operator checks for the presence of rows in a subquery. It returns TRUE as soon as the subquery returns its first record, making it highly efficient for existence checks.","A describes the IN operator. C describes a data dictionary check. D is an aggregate function.","A describes the IN operator. C describes a data dictionary check. D is an aggregate function."]},{"question_text":"Given a relation R(A, B, C, D) with functional dependencies A -> B and B -> C. What is the closure of attribute A?","options":["The attribute set {A, B}","The attribute set {A, B, C}","","with functional dependencies A -> B and B -> C. What is the closure of attribute A?A) The attribute set {A, B}B) The attribute set {A, B, C}C) The attribute set {A, B, C, D}D) The attribute set {A}"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A and D are incomplete subsets. C incorrectly includes D, which cannot be derived from A using the given dependencies.","Starting with A, the dependency A -> B allows us to add B, giving {A, B}. The dependency B -> C then allows us to add C, giving {A, B, C}. Since there are no dependencies starting with C or combinations of ABC that point to D, the closure stops there.","A and D are incomplete subsets. C incorrectly includes D, which cannot be derived from A using the given dependencies.","A and D are incomplete subsets. C incorrectly includes D, which cannot be derived from A using the given dependencies."]},{"question_text":"What is a primary limitation of conventional file-processing systems compared to a modern DBMS?","options":["Extremely slow processing speeds for single, isolated datasets.","Exceedingly high initial setup and hardware installation costs.","High data redundancy and potential data inconsistency across duplicate files.","Complete lack of simple password protection options."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A and B are potential advantages of file systems for small, isolated tasks. D is incorrect because basic file-level security features do exist.","A and B are potential advantages of file systems for small, isolated tasks. D is incorrect because basic file-level security features do exist.","In conventional file systems, duplicate files are often created across different departments, leading to data redundancy, which naturally breeds data inconsistency when one file is updated and another is not.","A and B are potential advantages of file systems for small, isolated tasks. D is incorrect because basic file-level security features do exist."]},{"question_text":"If a decomposition of relation R into sub-relations R1 and R2 ensures that the intersection of R1 and R2 functionally determines R1, what property is guaranteed?","options":["Dependency Preservation Property","Lossless-Join Decomposition Property","Redundancy Minimization Property","Higher Normal Form Property"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A deals with preserving functional dependencies across tables. C and D describe the goals of normalization rather than this specific structural check.","For a decomposition to be lossless, the attributes shared between the two sub-relations must act as a super key for at least one of the sub-relations. Here, the shared attributes determine all attributes in R1, making it a super key for R1 and guaranteeing a lossless join.","A deals with preserving functional dependencies across tables. C and D describe the goals of normalization rather than this specific structural check.","A deals with preserving functional dependencies across tables. C and D describe the goals of normalization rather than this specific structural check."]},{"question_text":"What does a double diamond represent in an ER diagram?","options":["A multivalued relationship link","An identifying relationship set that links a weak entity to a strong entity","A total participation link between strong entities","A recursive loop relationship"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D are not standard interpretations for a double diamond.","In standard Chen ER notation, the relationship linking a weak entity set to its strong owner entity set is called an identifying relationship and is enclosed in a double-lined diamond.","A, C, and D are not standard interpretations for a double diamond.","A, C, and D are not standard interpretations for a double diamond."]},{"question_text":"Consider the following SQL query:\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM Employees WHERE Salary > ANY (SELECT Salary FROM Employees WHERE DeptID = 10);\n```\n\nWhat does this query return?","options":["Employees who earn more than every employee in department 10.","Employees who earn more than the average salary of department 10.","Employees who earn more than at least one employee in department 10 (i.e., more than the lowest earner in department 10).","A syntax error because ANY cannot be used with a greater-than operator."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A describes the > ALL operator. B describes comparing against an AVG() subquery. D is false because this is standard SQL syntax.","A describes the > ALL operator. B describes comparing against an AVG() subquery. D is false because this is standard SQL syntax.","The > ANY operator requires the outer value to be larger than at least one value in the subquery''s result set, which is logically equivalent to being greater than the minimum value in that set.","A describes the > ALL operator. B describes comparing against an AVG() subquery. D is false because this is standard SQL syntax."]}]'::jsonb,
